const T=[
/* METRICS */
["North Star Metric","metrics","The one number that best shows users are getting real value from the product.","A single outcome metric that captures delivered customer value and predicts long-term revenue. It sits above a tree of input metrics teams can directly move.","Spotify: time spent listening. Airbnb: nights booked. Blinkit: orders delivered on time and complete.","“I treat the North Star as the outcome, and I look for the input metric my design can move, like add-to-cart rate, that feeds it.”"],
["Input and output metrics","metrics","Output is the result you want; inputs are the levers that cause it.","Output (lagging) metrics such as revenue or retention cannot be moved directly. Input (controllable) metrics, such as onboarding completion, are what a team owns and moves week to week.","Output: monthly orders. Inputs: search success rate, checkout completion, delivery ETA accuracy.","“Revenue is the output, so for this redesign I’d own checkout completion as my input metric.”"],
["Vanity metric","metrics","A number that looks impressive but tells you nothing about whether the product works.","A metric that rises regardless of product quality and cannot inform a decision: cumulative totals, raw downloads, page views without context.","“1 million downloads” while 90% of those users never open the app a second time.","“Total signups is a vanity metric here. I’d look at week-one retention instead.”"],
["Actionable metric","metrics","A number that tells you what to do next.","A metric tied to a specific behaviour, segmented and comparable over time, so a change in it maps to a decision.","Checkout drop-off at the address step, split by new and returning users.","“I prefer metrics we can act on, so I’d break conversion down by step.”"],
["Leading and lagging indicators","metrics","Leading numbers warn you early; lagging numbers confirm what already happened.","Leading indicators predict future outcomes and move quickly (activation, feature adoption). Lagging indicators are final and slow (churn, revenue, NPS).","Activation in week one is a leading indicator of 90-day retention.","“Retention lags by a month, so I’d watch activation as the leading signal after launch.”"],
["Guardrail metric","metrics","A number you promise not to break while improving another.","A counter-metric monitored during an experiment to catch harmful side effects of optimising the primary metric.","Pushing more add-ons at checkout lifts order value, but the guardrail is cancellation and refund rate.","“The primary metric is conversion; my guardrails are refund rate and support tickets.”"],
["HEART framework","metrics","Google’s five ways to measure user experience: Happiness, Engagement, Adoption, Retention, Task success.","A UX metrics framework paired with Goals-Signals-Metrics: for each HEART category define the goal, the observable signal, then the metric.","Task success for search: goal is finding an item, signal is a tap on a result, metric is % of searches ending in add-to-cart.","“I’d use HEART with goals, signals and metrics so we don’t just pick numbers we happen to have.”"],
["AARRR (pirate metrics)","metrics","The user lifecycle in five steps: Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Referral, Revenue.","A funnel framework by Dave McClure for locating where a product leaks users. Each stage gets its own metric and owner.","A fintech app acquiring well but losing users before the first payment has an activation problem.","“Mapping this on AARRR, the leak is activation, not acquisition, so the onboarding is where design matters most.”"],
["DAU/MAU (stickiness)","metrics","How many of your monthly users come back daily.","Daily active users divided by monthly active users. Around 20% is decent for most apps, 50%+ is exceptional (messaging).","WhatsApp is near the top; a tax-filing app is near the bottom, and that is fine for its use case.","“Stickiness has to be judged against natural frequency. A travel app shouldn’t chase daily use.”"],
["Retention curve","metrics","A chart of how many users are still active after day 1, day 7, day 30.","Percentage of a cohort still active over time. The key signal is whether the curve flattens (product-market fit) or keeps falling to zero.","A curve flattening at 25% by week six means a core group found lasting value.","“The curve flattens around week four, so the work is moving more people into that retained group earlier.”"],
["Churn rate","metrics","The share of users or customers who leave in a period.","Lost customers ÷ customers at start of period. Voluntary churn (chose to leave) differs from involuntary churn (payment failed).","A subscription loses 5% of members a month: about 46% gone in a year.","“Part of that churn is involuntary, failed payments, which is a design fix: card-update reminders.”"],
["Conversion rate","metrics","The percentage of people who do the thing you wanted.","Completions of a target action ÷ eligible users, always defined with a clear denominator and time window.","Of 1,000 people viewing a product page, 40 buy: 4% conversion.","“I’d define conversion from cart-view to order placed, not from app open, so the number reflects checkout design.”"],
["Funnel and drop-off","metrics","The step-by-step path to a goal, and where people quit.","A funnel measures sequential step completion; drop-off is the loss between steps. The biggest absolute drop is usually the first design target.","Cart 100% → address 70% → payment 55% → success 50%. Address is the biggest leak.","“The largest drop is at address entry, so I’d start with saved addresses and pin-drop location.”"],
["Bounce rate","metrics","People who land on a page and leave without doing anything.","Single-interaction sessions ÷ total sessions. High bounce can mean mismatch between promise (ad, search result) and page.","A landing page from an ad bounces at 80% because the headline doesn’t match the ad.","“High bounce here looks like a message mismatch, not a layout problem.”"],
["NPS (Net Promoter Score)","metrics","“How likely are you to recommend us?” scored 0–10.","% promoters (9–10) minus % detractors (0–6), range −100 to +100. Useful as a relationship trend, weak as a design metric because it’s distant from any single change.","An NPS of +40 is strong for fintech; the open-text reason is more useful than the score.","“NPS is a good health trend, but I wouldn’t judge a single redesign on it. Task success is closer to the change.”"],
["CSAT","metrics","A quick “How satisfied were you?” rating right after an interaction.","Customer Satisfaction Score: share of 4–5 responses on a 1–5 scale, collected at a specific touchpoint.","A rating prompt after a support chat closes.","“CSAT works at a touchpoint, so I’d trigger it right after the refund flow, not on app open.”"],
["CES (Customer Effort Score)","metrics","“How easy was it to get this done?”","A transactional metric measuring perceived effort; strongly correlated with loyalty in service flows.","After a return request: “How easy was it to return this item?”","“For a returns flow, effort is the right measure. Satisfaction with a return is the wrong question.”"],
["SUS (System Usability Scale)","metrics","A standard 10-question survey that gives a usability score out of 100.","Alternating positive/negative Likert items normalised to 0–100. About 68 is average; above 80 is good. Comparable across products and versions.","Run SUS after usability tests of version A and B to compare them.","“We scored 62 on SUS before and 78 after, measured with the same tasks.”"],
["Task success rate","metrics","The share of test participants who completed the task.","Binary or graded completion in usability testing, often reported with confidence intervals because samples are small.","4 of 5 participants found the refund option unaided.","“Task success went from 2 of 5 to 5 of 5 after we moved refunds into order details.”"],
["Time on task","metrics","How long it takes to finish something.","Duration from task start to completion in testing or analytics. Lower is better for utility tasks; context-dependent for content.","Adding a new payee took 94 seconds; the target was under 45.","“Time on task matters for repeat flows like reordering, where speed is the whole value.”"],
["Error rate","metrics","How often users make mistakes during a task.","Count of slips (wrong tap) and mistakes (wrong mental model) per task attempt.","Users repeatedly tap the inactive “Pay” button before the amount is entered.","“Those are mistakes, not slips. The model is wrong, so a bigger button won’t fix it.”"],
/* STRATEGY */
["Jobs To Be Done (JTBD)","strategy","People “hire” a product to get a job done in their life.","A framework (Christensen, Ulwick) that defines need as a progress a user wants in a circumstance, independent of solution: “When [situation], I want to [motivation], so I can [outcome].”","“When I’m cooking and run out of an ingredient, I want it in 10 minutes, so I don’t abandon the meal.” That is Blinkit’s job.","“I framed the problem as a job statement first, so the solution wasn’t tied to our current feature set.”"],
["Product-market fit (PMF)","strategy","The product solves a real problem for enough people who will keep using or paying for it.","Evidenced by flattening retention, organic growth and the Sean Ellis test (40%+ would be “very disappointed” without it).","Early Slack: teams pulled it in themselves and wouldn’t go back to email.","“Before PMF I’d optimise for learning speed; after it, for scale and polish.”"],
["Value proposition","strategy","The clear reason someone picks you over the alternative.","A statement of target user, the job, the benefit and the differentiator against the next best option (including doing nothing).","CRED: pay credit-card bills and get rewarded for it.","“The value proposition has to show up in the first screen, not on page three of onboarding.”"],
["MVP","strategy","The smallest thing you can build to learn whether the idea works.","Minimum Viable Product: optimised for validated learning, not for launch quality. Viable means it really delivers the core value.","Zappos started by photographing shoes in local shops and buying them by hand after each order.","“For the MVP I’d cut the comparison view and test only whether people trust the recommendation.”"],
["MLP and MMP","strategy","MLP is the smallest version people love; MMP is the smallest version you can sell.","Minimum Lovable Product adds the emotional quality needed for retention; Minimum Marketable Product adds what’s required to launch to a market (billing, support, legal).","A habit app’s MLP adds a satisfying streak animation the MVP skipped.","“An MVP tests the bet; the MLP is what we ship publicly so the first impression doesn’t kill retention.”"],
["Problem statement","strategy","A clear sentence of who has what problem and why it matters.","A scoped definition: user, need, insight, and impact. It sets boundaries for ideation and how success will be judged.","“First-time UPI users abandon payments after a failure because they can’t tell whether money left their account.”","“Before sketching, I’d agree the problem statement with the PM so we judge ideas against the same thing.”"],
["How Might We (HMW)","strategy","Turning a problem into an open question that invites ideas.","A reframing technique from IDEO: broad enough to allow many solutions, narrow enough to be actionable.","“How might we make a failed payment feel safe instead of scary?”","“I turned the top insights into three HMW questions and ran ideation against each.”"],
["Double Diamond","strategy","Explore the problem widely, focus it, then explore solutions widely, then focus.","Design Council model: Discover → Define (problem diamond), Develop → Deliver (solution diamond). Diverge and converge twice.","Research many causes of cart abandonment, define one, sketch many fixes, ship one.","“We were jumping into the second diamond too early, so I pushed for a week of discovery.”"],
["Design thinking","strategy","A people-first way of solving problems: empathise, define, ideate, prototype, test.","Stanford d.school’s five modes. Iterative rather than linear; prototypes are for learning, not presenting.","Shadowing delivery partners before redesigning their app.","“I use it as a loop. Testing often sends me back to redefine the problem.”"],
["Lean UX","strategy","Build small, measure, learn, repeat. Fewer documents, more experiments.","Jeff Gothelf’s approach: outcomes over deliverables, shared understanding over handoff documents, hypotheses over requirements.","A one-week prototype test instead of a 40-page spec.","“I’d rather run a scrappy test this week than polish a spec nobody has validated.”"],
["Hypothesis-driven design","strategy","Treat every design idea as a guess you can prove wrong.","“We believe [change] for [user] will result in [outcome]. We’ll know we’re right when we see [metric].”","We believe showing refund status on the failure screen will cut support tickets by 20%.","“I wrote it as a hypothesis with a success metric, so we’d know whether to keep it.”"],
["Opportunity solution tree","strategy","A map from the business outcome to user problems to ideas to tests.","Teresa Torres’s tool: desired outcome → opportunities (needs, pains) → solutions → assumption tests. Prevents jumping from goal straight to feature.","Outcome: raise reorders. Opportunity: users forget what they bought. Solutions: reorder shelf, smart list.","“I mapped it as an opportunity tree so we could see three problems behind the metric, not one feature.”"],
["Riskiest assumption","strategy","The belief that, if wrong, sinks the whole idea. Test it first.","Assumption mapping plots assumptions by importance and evidence; the riskiest assumption test (RAT) targets the top-right corner before building.","A meal-kit idea assumes people will plan meals a week ahead. Test that before designing recipes.","“The riskiest assumption is that users trust auto-pay, so my first test is a fake-door on that.”"],
["Vision, strategy, roadmap","strategy","Where we’re going, how we’ll win, and what we’ll build in what order.","Vision is the long-term change in the world; strategy is the chosen path and trade-offs; roadmap is sequenced bets, often as now / next / later.","Vision: nobody runs out of essentials. Strategy: dark stores in 10-minute radius. Roadmap: reorder, then subscriptions.","“This feature fits the strategy, but it’s a next, not a now.”"],
["Blue ocean","strategy","Create a market with no competition instead of fighting in a crowded one.","Kim and Mauborgne: raise and create factors competitors ignore, reduce and eliminate the ones they over-serve.","Nintendo Wii skipped graphics power and created motion gaming for families.","“Instead of matching every competitor feature, I’d pick one dimension nobody serves well.”"],
["10x vs 10%","strategy","Is this a small improvement, or a step change that changes behaviour?","Incremental optimisation versus a solution an order of magnitude better, the only kind that makes users switch from an entrenched habit.","UPI wasn’t a slightly better card swipe; it removed cards entirely.","“A 10% better form won’t move adoption. The 10x idea is skipping the form with autofill.”"],
/* PRIORITY */
["RICE","priority","Score ideas by Reach, Impact, Confidence and Effort.","(Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort. Intercom’s model; confidence is what keeps exciting but unproven ideas honest.","Reach 5,000/month × impact 2 × confidence 80% ÷ 2 person-weeks = 4,000.","“I scored them with RICE, but I lowered confidence on the second idea because it’s untested.”"],
["ICE","priority","A faster score: Impact, Confidence, Ease.","Each 1–10, multiplied or averaged. Common in growth teams for ranking many small experiments quickly.","Ranking 20 onboarding experiments in an hour.","“For a long list of quick tests, ICE is enough. RICE is for bigger bets.”"],
["MoSCoW","priority","Must have, Should have, Could have, Won’t have this time.","A scope negotiation tool for a fixed release. The Won’t column is the important one: it records what was knowingly cut.","For v1: must have pay, should have split bill, could have themes, won’t have international.","“Let’s agree the won’t-haves now so they don’t creep back in mid-sprint.”"],
["Kano model","priority","Some features are expected, some get better with more, some surprise and delight.","Classifies features as Basic (absence angers), Performance (linear satisfaction), Delighter (unexpected), Indifferent, Reverse. Delighters decay into basics over time.","Order tracking was a delighter in 2015 and is a basic expectation now.","“Live tracking is a basic now, so it can’t be our differentiator.”"],
["Impact/effort matrix","priority","A 2×2 to find quick wins and avoid big costly bets with little payoff.","Plot items by value and cost: quick wins, major projects, fill-ins, money pits.","Saved addresses: high impact, low effort. A custom map engine: high effort, low impact.","“Saved addresses is a quick win, so I’d ship it while we research the bigger checkout rethink.”"],
["OKRs","priority","An Objective you want, and 2–4 measurable Key Results that prove you got there.","Objectives are qualitative and ambitious; key results are numeric outcomes, not tasks. Often graded 0–1, with 0.7 considered success.","O: make paying bills effortless. KR: bill-pay time under 30 s; repeat use up 15%.","“That’s an output, a task. The key result should be the outcome it changes.”"],
["KPI","priority","A number the business watches continuously to know it’s healthy.","Key Performance Indicator: standing operational metric, unlike OKRs, which are time-boxed goals.","Average delivery time is a KPI for quick commerce.","“Delivery time is a KPI we must protect; my OKR this quarter is reorder rate.”"],
["Agile and Scrum","priority","Build in short cycles, show working software often, adjust as you learn.","Agile is the philosophy; Scrum is a framework with sprints, a product owner, a scrum master, and ceremonies: planning, stand-up, review, retro.","Two-week sprints with a demo every second Friday.","“I work a sprint ahead of engineering so designs are tested before they’re picked up.”"],
["Sprint","priority","A fixed block of time, usually two weeks, to finish a set of work.","A time-boxed iteration with a committed scope and a shippable increment at the end.","Sprint 14: payment failure screen and retry flow.","“Can we split this so the error state lands this sprint and the animation next?”"],
["Kanban","priority","A board of cards moving through columns, with limits on work in progress.","Continuous flow instead of sprints; WIP limits expose bottlenecks.","Design columns: to explore, in progress, in review, ready for dev.","“Our review column keeps piling up, so a WIP limit there would help.”"],
["Backlog","priority","The ordered list of everything the team might build.","Product backlog items prioritised by the product owner; refined (groomed) regularly so top items are ready.","Top of the backlog: bulk delete, then dark mode, then CSV export.","“That idea is valid, so let’s add it to the backlog rather than the sprint.”"],
["User story and acceptance criteria","priority","A feature written from the user’s side, plus the conditions that make it done.","“As a [user], I want [goal], so that [benefit].” Acceptance criteria often in Given / When / Then form.","Given payment failed, when the user opens the order, then they see refund status and ETA.","“I added acceptance criteria for the empty and error states so QA tests them too.”"],
["Epic","priority","A big piece of work that’s split into many stories.","A large body of work spanning several sprints, broken into user stories.","Epic: subscriptions. Stories: choose plan, pause, cancel, change date.","“I’d design the whole epic’s flow first, then hand off story by story.”"],
["Scope creep","priority","The project keeps growing after it was agreed.","Uncontrolled addition of requirements without adjusting time or resources. Feature creep is the product-level version.","A simple bill-split turns into group chat, polls and reminders.","“That’s scope creep. Can we ship the split first and test whether people want the rest?”"],
["Opportunity cost","priority","Whatever you give up by choosing this.","The value of the best alternative not chosen. Every roadmap yes is several nos.","Two sprints on animations means two sprints not spent on the failing checkout.","“The real cost of this polish is the checkout work we’d delay.”"],
/* SLANG */
["Painkiller","slang","A product that removes a sharp, urgent pain. People go looking for it.","High-urgency problem, clear willingness to pay, low marketing cost. Usually easier to sell and retain.","Uber when it’s raining and no auto will stop.","“This is a painkiller for people who’ve had a payment fail, so the entry point should be the failure itself.”"],
["Vitamin","slang","Nice to have. Good for you, but nobody panics without it.","Low-urgency value; adoption depends on habit-building and is easy to churn from. Can become a painkiller once a habit forms.","A mood journal, a step counter.","“Honestly this is a vitamin, so it needs a habit loop or it won’t survive week two.”"],
["Candy","slang","Fun and addictive, but no lasting value.","Engagement driven by reward without underlying user benefit. Spikes then decays.","A viral filter app that’s everywhere for a month.","“The streak makes it candy. I’d rather tie the reward to real progress.”"],
["Toothbrush test","slang","Will people use it once or twice every day?","Larry Page’s filter for products worth building or buying: frequent, essential use.","Search, maps and messaging pass it; a wedding planner doesn’t, and that’s fine.","“It fails the toothbrush test, so the model must be high value per use, not daily engagement.”"],
["Band-aid fix","slang","A quick patch that hides the symptom but leaves the cause.","Short-term mitigation. Acceptable when labelled and time-boxed; harmful when it becomes permanent design debt.","Adding a tooltip to explain a confusing button instead of renaming it.","“A tooltip is a band-aid. The real fix is a label people understand.”"],
["Bikeshedding","slang","Spending ages on easy, trivial decisions and skipping the hard ones.","Parkinson’s law of triviality: people debate what they understand (button colour) and avoid what they don’t (data model, IA).","An hour on icon style, five minutes on the checkout structure.","“We’re bikeshedding. Can we settle the flow first and come back to colour?”"],
["Boiling the ocean","slang","Trying to solve everything at once.","Over-broad scope that prevents shipping or learning.","Redesigning the whole app instead of the failing checkout.","“Let’s not boil the ocean. One flow, one metric, two weeks.”"],
["Cargo culting","slang","Copying what a famous product does without knowing why it worked for them.","Imitating surface patterns without the underlying conditions.","Adding Stories to a banking app because Instagram has them.","“That pattern works for Instagram because of daily content. We don’t have that.”"],
["Gold plating","slang","Polishing beyond what anyone needs.","Extra quality or features beyond requirements, adding cost and delay without value.","Custom illustrations for an internal admin tool used by four people.","“That’s gold plating for v1. Ship the standard component.”"],
["Desire path / paving the cow path","slang","The shortcut users invent. Good design formalises it.","Observed behaviour diverging from designed flows; paving it means supporting what users already do.","Users screenshotting their cart to share it, so you add a share-cart button.","“People already share carts by screenshot. Let’s pave that path.”"],
["Dogfooding","slang","Using your own product for real, every day.","Internal usage before release to catch issues and build empathy.","The team orders their office snacks through the app they build.","“We dogfooded it for a week and found the slot picker broke at midnight.”"],
["Zombie feature","slang","A feature nobody uses that nobody removes.","Low-adoption features that add maintenance and cognitive cost. Sunsetting them is a design decision too.","A “themes” setting used by 0.3% of users.","“Usage says it’s a zombie. I’d sunset it and reclaim the space.”"],
["Frankendesign","slang","A product stitched from mismatched parts.","Inconsistent patterns from different teams and eras, without a shared system.","Three different date pickers in one app.","“We have three date pickers, which is a sign we need a system component.”"],
["Feature fatigue","slang","So many features the product gets harder to use.","Capability looks attractive at purchase; usability suffers in use (Thompson, Hamilton, Rust).","A remote with 50 buttons where people use five.","“Each new option has a cost for everyone who doesn’t need it.”"],
["Table stakes","slang","The minimum you need to even compete.","Expected features with no differentiation value; missing them disqualifies you.","Order tracking in a delivery app.","“Tracking is table stakes. Our edge has to be somewhere else.”"],
["Moving the needle","slang","Making a noticeable difference to a metric that matters.","Impact large enough to show in the key metric, not just in a local measure.","Fixing a 30% drop-off at address entry moves the needle; recolouring the button might not.","“Which of these actually moves the needle on conversion?”"],
["Low-hanging fruit","slang","Easy wins worth taking first.","High-value, low-effort fixes. Useful early, risky if they become the whole roadmap.","Fixing a confusing label that causes 20% of support tickets.","“Two of these are low-hanging fruit we can ship this week.”"],
["Rabbit hole","slang","A problem that eats far more time than it’s worth.","An open-ended investigation without bounds; Shape Up calls out rabbit holes before work starts.","Two days tuning an easing curve nobody asked about.","“I’d time-box it so we don’t go down a rabbit hole.”"],
/* RESEARCH */
["Persona","research","A research-based portrait of a type of user.","An archetype synthesised from real data: goals, behaviours, context, frustrations. Demographics alone don’t make a persona.","“Riya, 28, orders groceries late at night after work, values speed over price.”","“The persona is built from 12 interviews, and the key trait is late-night urgency, not age.”"],
["Proto-persona","research","A quick persona made from team assumptions, to be tested later.","Hypothesis artefact created before research to align the team and expose assumptions.","A workshop sketch of “the budget student” before any interviews.","“This is a proto-persona, so treat it as assumptions we still need to check.”"],
["Empathy map","research","What the user says, thinks, does and feels.","A four-quadrant synthesis tool, often with pains and gains, used to align teams on a user segment.","Says “it’s fine”, does triple-check the bank app after every payment.","“The gap between what they say and do was the insight: they don’t trust the success screen.”"],
["Customer journey map","research","The user’s experience step by step across every touchpoint, including feelings.","Stages, actions, touchpoints, thoughts, emotional curve and opportunities over time, often across channels.","From hearing about the app to the first refund call with support.","“The journey map showed the lowest emotion was after paying, while waiting, not during checkout.”"],
["Service blueprint","research","A journey map that also shows what happens behind the scenes.","Adds frontstage staff, backstage processes, support systems and the line of visibility below the customer journey.","A refund: what the user sees, what support does, what the bank API does.","“The failure isn’t in the UI. The blueprint shows refunds wait on a batch job.”"],
["User interview","research","A one-on-one conversation to understand someone’s behaviour and needs.","Semi-structured, open questions about past behaviour (“Tell me about the last time…”), not hypothetical future intent.","“Walk me through the last time a payment failed.”","“I ask about the last time it happened, never ‘would you use this’.”"],
["Contextual inquiry","research","Watching and asking questions while people do the task in their real setting.","Master–apprentice model of field research; reveals workarounds and environment that interviews miss.","Sitting with a kirana owner during evening rush.","“In context we saw the shopkeeper uses the app one-handed with a customer waiting.”"],
["Diary study","research","Users log their experience over days or weeks.","Longitudinal self-reported research capturing behaviour in context over time.","Two weeks of logging every grocery run and why.","“A diary study caught the weekly pattern a single interview never would.”"],
["Card sorting","research","Users group topics into categories that make sense to them.","Open (users name groups), closed (predefined groups) or hybrid; informs information architecture.","Where would users file “invoices”: Account, Payments or Documents?","“An open card sort gave us the category names users actually use.”"],
["Tree testing","research","Testing whether people can find things in your menu structure, without any visual design.","Reverse card sort: users navigate a text hierarchy to complete find tasks; measures success and directness.","“Find where you’d change your delivery address.”","“The tree test showed 60% looked under Profile, so we moved it.”"],
["Usability testing","research","Watching real people try to complete tasks with your design.","Task-based evaluation, moderated (live facilitator) or unmoderated (remote tool), measuring success, time, errors and observed friction.","Five users try to request a refund in the prototype.","“Five moderated sessions found the three biggest issues; I’d run unmoderated tests to size them.”"],
["Think-aloud protocol","research","Asking users to say what they’re thinking while they use the product.","Concurrent verbalisation reveals expectations and confusion in the moment.","“I’m looking for… I guess this means cancel?”","“The think-aloud showed they read ‘Hold’ as ‘Cancel’.”"],
["Guerrilla testing","research","Quick, informal tests with people you find nearby.","Low-cost, fast feedback; weak on recruiting the right users, good for obvious problems.","Showing a prototype to people at a cafe for five minutes each.","“For first-impression issues, guerrilla tests are enough. For domain flows I recruit real users.”"],
["Five-user rule","research","Testing with about five users finds most usability problems.","Nielsen and Landauer: ~85% of issues found with five users per distinct segment, for qualitative testing only.","Five per persona, then fix and test again.","“Five users is for finding problems, not for proving a metric.”"],
["Affinity mapping","research","Grouping research notes into themes.","Bottom-up clustering of observations into patterns, then insights.","200 sticky notes become six themes, one of which is trust.","“I clustered the notes and the trust theme had the most evidence across participants.”"],
["Observation vs insight","research","An observation is what happened; an insight is why it matters.","Insight combines observation, cause and implication for design.","Observation: users screenshot the payment screen. Insight: they don’t trust the app’s record as proof.","“The insight isn’t that they screenshot, it’s that our receipt doesn’t feel like proof.”"],
["Heuristic evaluation","research","Experts reviewing a design against usability principles.","Several evaluators independently audit against a heuristic set (usually Nielsen’s 10) and rate severity.","Three designers audit checkout and log 23 issues by severity.","“A heuristic review is fast and cheap, so I use it before testing to clear the obvious problems.”"],
["Competitive analysis","research","Studying how others solve the same problem.","Systematic comparison of competitors and adjacent products on features, flows, patterns and positioning.","Comparing failure screens across GPay, PhonePe and Paytm.","“I looked at competitors to learn the conventions users already expect, not to copy screens.”"],
["Qualitative vs quantitative","research","Qual tells you why; quant tells you how many.","Qualitative: interviews, tests, small samples, depth. Quantitative: analytics, surveys, A/B tests, scale.","Analytics: 40% drop at address. Interviews: because the map pin is confusing.","“The data told us where. The interviews told us why.”"],
["Triangulation","research","Checking a finding with more than one method.","Corroboration across data sources or methods increases confidence.","Support tickets, analytics and interviews all point to refund anxiety.","“Three sources agree, so I’m confident enough to prioritise it.”"],
["Research biases","research","Ways research gets distorted without anyone noticing.","Leading questions, confirmation bias, social desirability, sampling bias and survivorship bias (only studying users who stayed).","“Don’t you love this feature?” is a leading question.","“We only talked to retained users, so I want to interview people who churned too.”"],
["Stakeholder interview","research","Talking to the people inside the company who shape the product.","Captures business goals, constraints, history and success measures from PMs, engineering, support, sales and leaders.","Support lead: “40% of tickets are refund status.”","“Before user research I interview stakeholders to understand the constraints.”"],
/* PSYCH */
["Mental model","psych","What users believe about how something works.","An internal representation formed by past experience. Designs that match it feel intuitive; mismatches cause errors.","Users expect “Delete” to go to a bin they can restore from.","“The confusion comes from a mismatched mental model, so I changed the system, not the tooltip.”"],
["Cognitive load","psych","How much thinking a screen asks for.","Intrinsic (task difficulty), extraneous (bad design) and germane (learning) load. Design removes extraneous load.","A form asking for address, GST, landmark and pin all at once.","“The task is hard enough, so I removed extraneous load by splitting the form.”"],
["Hick’s law","psych","More choices make decisions slower.","Decision time grows logarithmically with the number of equally likely choices.","A menu with 30 options versus a grouped menu of five.","“Hick’s law is why I grouped the 14 payment options into three.”"],
["Fitts’s law","psych","Bigger, closer targets are faster to hit.","Movement time depends on distance to and size of the target. Edges and corners act as infinitely large targets on desktop.","The pay button sits large and at the bottom, in thumb reach.","“By Fitts’s law the primary action should be big and near the thumb.”"],
["Jakob’s law","psych","People expect your product to work like the others they use.","Users spend most time on other sites, so convention beats novelty for common tasks.","The cart icon sits top-right because everyone puts it there.","“I kept the standard checkout pattern. Novelty here would only cost us.”"],
["Miller’s law","psych","People can only hold a few things in short-term memory.","Originally 7±2, now believed closer to about 4 chunks. Basis for chunking information.","Phone numbers split as 98110 23456.","“I chunked the account number so it can be checked at a glance.”"],
["Peak-end rule","psych","People remember the best or worst moment and the ending.","Kahneman: experiences are judged by peak intensity and the final moment, not the average.","A rough delivery saved by a kind note and instant refund at the end.","“The ending of the refund flow is what they’ll remember, so it gets the most care.”"],
["Paradox of choice","psych","Too many options make people anxious and less satisfied.","Schwartz: choice overload reduces decisions and satisfaction.","50 kinds of milk; most people want the one they bought last time.","“I’d default to the last-ordered variant and hide the rest behind ‘more’.”"],
["Loss aversion","psych","Losing hurts about twice as much as gaining pleases.","Prospect theory. Framing as a loss motivates more strongly; used ethically and unethically.","“You’ll lose your 450 reward points” beats “Earn 450 points”.","“Loss framing works, but on a cancel screen it tips into a dark pattern.”"],
["Anchoring","psych","The first number seen shapes how all later numbers feel.","Initial reference values bias judgement, common in pricing displays.","A struck-through ₹999 makes ₹599 feel cheap.","“Showing the annual plan first anchors the monthly price as expensive.”"],
["Serial position effect","psych","People remember the first and last items in a list best.","Primacy and recency effects; the middle is forgotten.","Most important tab bar items at the two ends.","“Home and account sit at the ends of the tab bar because of serial position.”"],
["Von Restorff effect","psych","The thing that stands out gets remembered.","Isolation effect: distinctive items are recalled better. Basis for one emphasised primary action.","One coloured “Recommended” plan among grey ones.","“If everything is highlighted, nothing is. One primary action per screen.”"],
["Zeigarnik effect","psych","Unfinished tasks stick in the mind.","Incomplete tasks are remembered better, which motivates completion.","“Profile 70% complete” nudges users to finish it.","“A visible progress state uses Zeigarnik to bring people back to finish setup.”"],
["Goal-gradient effect","psych","People speed up as they get close to a goal.","Motivation increases with proximity to the reward; giving a head start helps.","A loyalty card that starts with 2 of 10 stamps filled.","“Starting the progress bar at step two of five uses goal gradient.”"],
["IKEA and endowment effect","psych","People value what they made or already own more.","Effort invested and ownership increase perceived value, which raises switching costs.","A playlist you built over years keeps you on Spotify.","“Letting users customise their home screen early builds ownership and retention.”"],
["Social proof","psych","People follow what others like them are doing.","Cialdini: uncertainty makes us copy others. Must be real to stay ethical.","“2,300 people in Noida ordered this today.”","“Social proof helps when it’s true and local. Invented numbers destroy trust.”"],
["Scarcity","psych","Things feel more valuable when they’re running out.","Limited supply or time increases desirability; fake scarcity is a dark pattern.","“Only 2 slots left for 7 pm delivery.”","“Real slot scarcity is useful information. Fake timers are manipulation.”"],
["Default effect","psych","Most people keep whatever is pre-selected.","Defaults are the most powerful choice-architecture tool; they should favour the user.","Organ donation rates soar with opt-out defaults.","“Most users never change defaults, so the default is the design decision.”"],
["Aesthetic-usability effect","psych","Good-looking designs are perceived as easier to use.","Users tolerate minor issues in attractive interfaces, which can also hide real problems in testing.","Testers praise a beautiful prototype that they couldn’t actually complete.","“Testers liked it, but task success says otherwise. Aesthetic-usability can mask problems.”"],
["Hook model","psych","How habits form: trigger, action, variable reward, investment.","Nir Eyal’s model for habit-forming products. Investment loads the next trigger.","Notification → open → new likes → post again.","“Our investment step is saving a list, which brings people back next week.”"],
["Nudge and choice architecture","psych","Designing how choices are presented to steer gently without forcing.","Thaler and Sunstein: order, defaults, framing and friction shape decisions while keeping freedom of choice.","Healthy items at eye level; tip default at 10%.","“A good nudge helps users do what they already want to do.”"],
["Banner blindness","psych","People ignore anything that looks like an ad.","Users skip banner-shaped, brightly boxed content, even when it’s important.","An important outage notice styled like a promo gets missed.","“Styling the alert like a promo banner is why nobody saw it.”"],
["Doherty threshold","psych","If the system responds within about 0.4 seconds, people stay in flow.","Response times under ~400 ms keep users engaged; above, attention breaks.","Instant feedback on tap, even before the server answers.","“If the server needs two seconds, the UI still has to answer within 400 ms.”"],
["Tesler’s law","psych","Every task has some complexity you can’t remove, only move.","Law of conservation of complexity: either the system absorbs it or the user does.","Auto-detecting the bank from the IFSC moves complexity from user to system.","“By Tesler’s law that complexity goes somewhere. I’d rather the system carries it.”"],
["Postel’s law","psych","Accept messy input; give back clean output.","Robustness principle: be liberal in what you accept, conservative in what you send.","Accept phone numbers with spaces, dashes or +91; display them one way.","“The form should accept any format, by Postel’s law. Rejecting a space is our problem, not theirs.”"],
["Occam’s razor","psych","The simplest solution that works is usually best.","Prefer the explanation or design with fewest assumptions and elements.","Removing a confirmation step that never caught a real error.","“The simplest version passed the test, so I removed the extra step.”"],
/* HEURISTICS */
["Nielsen’s 10 heuristics","heuristics","Ten rules of thumb for usable interfaces.","System status; match with real world; user control; consistency; error prevention; recognition over recall; flexibility; minimalist design; error recovery; help.","Used to audit a checkout in an hour before testing.","“I ran a heuristic pass first. The main violations were system status and error recovery.”"],
["Visibility of system status","heuristics","Always tell users what is happening.","Timely, appropriate feedback about the system’s state. The first of Nielsen’s heuristics.","“Payment processing. Don’t close the app.” with a progress state.","“The problem is silence. The user can’t tell whether the payment is processing or stuck.”"],
["Recognition over recall","heuristics","Show options instead of making people remember them.","Minimise memory load by making elements, actions and options visible.","Recent searches and recent payees shown on tap.","“Showing recent payees turns recall into recognition.”"],
["Error prevention","heuristics","Design so mistakes can’t happen in the first place.","Constraints, good defaults and confirmation for slips; better than great error messages.","Disabling dates in the past in a booking calendar.","“Better to prevent the wrong date than write a good error for it.”"],
["User control and freedom","heuristics","Give a clear way out and a way to undo.","Emergency exits, undo and redo, cancel. Users make mistakes and need an easy reversal.","Undo after archiving an email.","“An undo is kinder than a confirmation dialog for most actions.”"],
["Consistency and standards","heuristics","Same thing, same look, same word, everywhere.","Internal consistency (within product) and external consistency (platform conventions).","“Remove” in one place and “Delete” in another for the same action.","“We call it three different things. Let’s pick one word.”"],
["Affordance","heuristics","How an object suggests what you can do with it.","Don Norman: the relationship between an object’s properties and a user’s capabilities that determines possible actions.","A raised button looks pressable; a handle looks pullable.","“The card has no affordance of being tappable, which is why people miss it.”"],
["Signifier","heuristics","A cue that tells you where and how to act.","Perceivable signals that communicate affordances: labels, icons, underlines, chevrons.","A chevron on a list row signals it opens.","“Adding a chevron is the signifier the row is missing.”"],
["Mapping","heuristics","Controls laid out to match what they control.","Natural mapping uses spatial correspondence between controls and effects.","Stove knobs arranged like the burners.","“The seat-map controls mirror the cabin layout, so it’s natural mapping.”"],
["Feedback","heuristics","Every action gets an immediate, clear response.","Communicates the result of an action; must be immediate, informative and proportionate.","A button changes state and shows a spinner on tap.","“The tap gets no feedback, so users tap three times and get charged twice.”"],
["Constraints (Norman)","heuristics","Limiting what users can do so they can only do the right thing.","Physical, logical, semantic and cultural constraints guide correct action.","A PIN field that accepts only digits.","“A numeric keyboard is a constraint that prevents the error entirely.”"],
["Dieter Rams’ principles","heuristics","Ten principles of good design, ending with “as little design as possible”.","Innovative, useful, aesthetic, understandable, unobtrusive, honest, long-lasting, thorough, environmentally friendly, minimal.","Braun products; often cited as the root of Apple’s design language.","“I lean on Rams’ ‘honest’ principle: the UI shouldn’t promise more than the system does.”"],
["Progressive disclosure","heuristics","Show only what’s needed now; reveal more when asked.","Defer advanced or rare features to secondary screens to reduce initial complexity.","“Advanced filters” behind a link; basic filters visible.","“Most people need three filters, so the rest sit behind progressive disclosure.”"],
/* VISUAL */
["Visual hierarchy","visual","The order in which the eye reads a screen.","Established through size, weight, colour, contrast, position and space. One clear first read per screen.","Amount due large, due date second, pay button third.","“The squint test shows the eye lands on the banner, not the amount. The hierarchy is wrong.”"],
["Gestalt: proximity","visual","Things close together look related.","Spacing is the strongest grouping cue, often stronger than colour or boxes.","A label sitting nearer its own field than the next one.","“The label is equidistant from two fields, so proximity is ambiguous.”"],
["Gestalt: similarity","visual","Things that look alike seem to belong together.","Shared colour, shape, size or style implies shared function.","All tappable links in one colour.","“That grey text looks like the disabled items, so by similarity people think it’s inactive.”"],
["Gestalt: common region","visual","Things inside the same box are seen as a group.","Shared enclosure groups elements, even if far apart.","Order summary inside one card.","“I used a common region for the bill details so they read as one unit.”"],
["Gestalt: figure-ground","visual","We separate the object in focus from the background.","Perception of foreground versus background; modals use a dimmed scrim to establish figure.","A bottom sheet over a dimmed screen.","“The scrim makes the sheet the figure, so attention goes where we want.”"],
["Gestalt: closure and continuity","visual","The brain completes shapes and follows smooth lines.","Closure fills gaps; continuity follows paths. Carousels that cut off a card signal more content.","A partly visible card at the screen edge says “swipe”.","“Letting the last card bleed off the edge signals scroll through closure.”"],
["Gestalt: common fate","visual","Things that move together seem related.","Elements moving in the same direction and speed are grouped.","Selected items lifting together when dragged.","“Animating the selected rows together uses common fate to show the group.”"],
["Whitespace","visual","Empty space that gives content room to breathe.","Negative space that groups, separates and signals importance; macro (layout) and micro (letter and line).","Luxury brands use more space around fewer items.","“I’m adding space, not boxes, to separate these groups.”"],
["Grid and 8-point system","visual","An invisible structure that aligns everything; spacing in steps of 8.","Columns, gutters and margins; spacing tokens in multiples of 8 (4 for small steps) for consistency across devices.","16, 24, 32, 48 px spacing steps; a 4-column mobile grid.","“All spacing comes from the 8-point scale, so engineers never guess.”"],
["Typography basics","visual","Choosing and setting type so it reads easily.","Type scale (ratio-based sizes), leading (line height), tracking (letter spacing), kerning (pairs), measure (45–75 characters per line), weight contrast.","Body 16/24, headings on a 1.25 scale, tighter tracking on large display text.","“I set a type scale with four sizes and let weight do the rest of the hierarchy.”"],
["Contrast (visual)","visual","Difference that makes something stand out.","Contrast in size, weight, colour, value and space creates hierarchy and readability.","A bold large total over a small light breakdown.","“The hierarchy is too flat. Everything is medium, so nothing leads.”"],
["Colour roles (60-30-10)","visual","Most of the screen in one colour, some in a second, a little accent.","A proportion heuristic: dominant neutral, secondary, and accent reserved for action and status; semantic colours for success, warning, error.","White ground, dark text, green used only for primary actions.","“Accent is reserved for the action. If marketing banners use it too, the button loses its meaning.”"],
["F-pattern and Z-pattern","visual","How people scan text-heavy pages (F) and sparse pages (Z).","Eye-tracking patterns: F for dense content (left edge, first lines), Z for simple layouts with few elements.","Search results follow an F; a landing page hero follows a Z.","“Since people scan in an F here, the first words of each row must carry the meaning.”"],
["Above the fold","visual","What’s visible before scrolling.","From newspapers. Important but often over-weighted; people do scroll if the first view promises more.","Amount, merchant and pay button all visible without scrolling.","“The essentials sit above the fold, but I’m not cramming everything there.”"],
["Responsive vs adaptive","visual","Responsive stretches fluidly; adaptive switches between fixed layouts.","Responsive uses fluid grids and media queries; adaptive serves distinct layouts at set breakpoints.","Responsive: cards reflow from 4 to 2 to 1 columns.","“I designed at three breakpoints but specified fluid behaviour between them.”"],
["Breakpoint","visual","The screen width where the layout changes.","Defined widths (for example 600, 900, 1200) where layout rules switch; ideally set by content, not devices.","At 600 px the side panel moves below the list.","“I set the breakpoint where the table stops fitting, not at an iPad width.”"],
["Visual style eras","visual","Skeuomorphism, flat, material, neumorphism, glassmorphism.","Skeuo mimics real materials; flat removes them; Material adds elevation; neumorphism uses soft extrusion (poor contrast); glass uses blur and transparency.","iOS 6 leather calendar (skeuo) versus iOS 7 (flat).","“Neumorphism fails contrast for buttons, so I avoid it for anything interactive.”"],
/* INTERACTION */
["Microinteraction","interaction","A tiny moment of feedback around a single action.","Dan Saffer: trigger, rules, feedback, loops and modes. Communicates state and adds personality.","The heart burst when liking a post; a toggle sliding.","“The microinteraction confirms the save without a toast interrupting the user.”"],
["Easing","interaction","How motion speeds up and slows down.","Acceleration curves. Ease-out for elements entering, ease-in for leaving, ease-in-out for moving on screen; linear feels mechanical.","A sheet sliding up with ease-out.","“Entrances use ease-out so the UI feels responsive, not sluggish.”"],
["Duration","interaction","How long an animation lasts.","Most UI transitions sit between 150 and 300 ms; larger distances and surfaces can go longer. Frequent actions should be fastest.","200 ms for a button press, 300 ms for a sheet.","“Anything people do 50 times a day should animate in under 200 ms, or not at all.”"],
["Spring animation","interaction","Motion that behaves like a physical spring, with a natural settle.","Physics-based animation defined by stiffness, damping and mass instead of duration; interruptible and velocity-aware, used heavily by Apple.","A card snapping back when you let go mid-swipe.","“A spring keeps the gesture’s velocity, so the sheet feels attached to your finger.”"],
["Choreography and stagger","interaction","Sequencing motion so elements arrive in a sensible order.","Offsets (often 30–60 ms) between related elements guide attention and convey hierarchy.","List items appear one after another, top to bottom.","“A small stagger leads the eye from title to content, but I keep it under 300 ms total.”"],
["Interaction states","interaction","How a control looks when idle, hovered, focused, pressed or disabled.","Default, hover, focus (keyboard), active/pressed, disabled, loading, selected. Each must be designed and specified.","A button darkens on press and shows a ring on keyboard focus.","“I’ve specified all six states for every control so engineering doesn’t invent them.”"],
["Thumb zone","interaction","The part of a phone screen your thumb reaches easily.","Steven Hoober’s research: most people hold phones one-handed; bottom-centre is easy, top corners are hard.","Primary actions at the bottom; rarely used settings at the top.","“The key action sits in the thumb zone, so it moves from the top bar to the bottom.”"],
["Interaction cost","interaction","The total effort to get something done: taps, reading, thinking, waiting.","Sum of mental and physical effort; fewer steps isn’t always lower cost if each is harder.","Three simple screens can beat one dense screen.","“The new flow has more taps but lower interaction cost, since each step is one decision.”"],
["Modal vs non-modal","interaction","A modal blocks everything until you deal with it; non-modal doesn’t.","Modals interrupt and demand a response; reserve them for critical decisions. Non-modal surfaces (toast, inline) keep flow.","A confirm-delete dialog is modal; a “Saved” toast is not.","“This doesn’t need a modal. An inline message keeps the user moving.”"],
["Toast and snackbar","interaction","A short message that pops up briefly and goes away.","Transient, non-modal feedback; snackbars may include one action like Undo. Never for errors users must act on.","“Item removed. Undo.”","“Errors that need action can’t be toasts, because they disappear before people read them.”"],
["Inline validation","interaction","Checking a form field and showing errors right next to it.","Real-time or on-blur validation with specific messages; avoid validating while the user is still typing.","“UPI ID should look like name@bank” under the field.","“Validate on blur, not on every keystroke, or users see errors before they’ve finished.”"],
["Smart defaults","interaction","Pre-filling the most likely answer.","Defaults inferred from context, history or majority behaviour reduce effort and errors.","Delivery address defaults to the last used one.","“90% reorder to the same address, so it’s the default, and changing it is one tap.”"],
["Forgiving formats","interaction","Letting people type data in any reasonable way.","Accept variations in input and normalise them, applying Postel’s law to forms.","Dates as 21/11, 21 Nov, or 21-11-2026 all accepted.","“The field shouldn’t reject a space in a card number. That’s our job to strip.”"],
["Undo vs confirm","interaction","Let people reverse an action instead of asking “Are you sure?”","Confirmations get clicked through by habit; undo respects flow and catches mistakes. Confirm only for irreversible, high-stakes actions.","Gmail’s Undo send instead of a confirm dialog.","“Confirmation dialogs train people to click yes. Undo is safer for most actions.”"],
["Onboarding patterns","interaction","Ways to teach a product to new users.","Product tours, coach marks, progressive (contextual) onboarding, checklists, empty-state teaching. Contextual beats upfront tours.","Teaching swipe-to-archive the first time the user opens their inbox.","“I replaced the five-screen tour with contextual tips at the moment of need.”"],
["Navigation patterns","interaction","How users move around an app.","Tab bar, hamburger drawer, top tabs, hub-and-spoke, breadcrumbs. Hidden navigation reduces discovery.","A 4-item bottom tab bar instead of a hamburger.","“A hamburger hides what we most want discovered, so I moved the key areas to the tab bar.”"],
["Bottom sheet","interaction","A panel that slides up from the bottom of the screen.","Modal or non-modal surface anchored to the bottom, often with detents (half, full); thumb-friendly.","Choosing a payment method without leaving checkout.","“A bottom sheet keeps context, so users don’t lose their cart.”"],
["Infinite scroll vs pagination","interaction","Endless loading as you scroll, or pages with numbers.","Infinite scroll suits browsing feeds; pagination or load-more suits search, comparison and reaching the footer.","A social feed scrolls forever; search results paginate.","“For comparison shopping, load-more beats infinite scroll, since people need to come back to items.”"],
["Gesture","interaction","Touch movements like swipe, pinch or long-press that trigger actions.","Gestures are invisible; they need signifiers, discoverable alternatives and consistent meaning across the app.","Swipe left to delete, with a visible delete option as well.","“Gestures are shortcuts, so every gesture also needs a visible button.”"],
["Haptic feedback","interaction","A small vibration that confirms an action.","Tactile feedback via the device’s motor; should be sparse and meaningful (success, warning, selection).","A light tap when a payment succeeds.","“One haptic on success. If everything buzzes, it stops meaning anything.”"],
/* STATES */
["Happy path","states","The ideal flow where everything goes right.","The default scenario with valid input, working network and existing data. Usually designed first and over-represented in portfolios.","User adds item, pays, order confirmed.","“That’s the happy path. Let me walk you through what happens when payment fails.”"],
["Unhappy path","states","What happens when things go wrong.","All failure branches: invalid input, timeouts, server errors, denied permissions, expired sessions.","Payment times out; the user doesn’t know if money was taken.","“I spent most of my time on the unhappy paths, because that’s where trust is lost.”"],
["Edge case","states","An unusual but real situation the design must still handle.","Valid inputs or conditions at the extremes: zero, one, many, very long, missing, slow.","A user with 0 orders, or with 4,000.","“I tested the layout with a 40-character merchant name and a ₹0 refund.”"],
["Corner case","states","Two or more edge cases happening at the same time.","Combinations of extreme conditions; rarer but often where bugs hide.","Offline and session expired while a payment is processing.","“The corner case is offline during payment, so we queue the status check for when the network returns.”"],
["Stress case","states","Designing for users in difficult real-life moments.","Sara Wachter-Boettcher and Eric Meyer: people under stress, grief or crisis use products too.","A “year in review” showing a photo of someone who has died.","“I considered stress cases: someone checking a failed payment while they’re stuck at a toll booth.”"],
["Empty state","states","What a screen shows when there’s no content yet.","Types: first-use, user-cleared, no-results, error-caused. Each should explain and offer the next action.","“No orders yet. Your first delivery is 10 minutes away.” with a Browse button.","“An empty state is onboarding in disguise, so it always has one clear action.”"],
["Loading state","states","What users see while content is coming.","Skeletons for known layouts, spinners for unknown short waits, progress bars for measurable processes; feedback within 100 ms.","A skeleton of the order card while it loads.","“Skeletons set expectations of what’s coming, so the wait feels shorter.”"],
["Skeleton screen","states","Grey placeholder shapes that mirror the layout before content arrives.","Content-aware placeholders that reduce perceived wait and layout shift.","YouTube’s grey boxes before thumbnails load.","“A skeleton that matches the real layout also prevents content jumping when it loads.”"],
["Optimistic UI","states","Showing an action as done before the server confirms it.","Update UI instantly, sync in background, roll back gracefully on failure. Not for money or irreversible actions.","A like appears instantly.","“Optimistic UI is fine for likes, never for payments.”"],
["Error state","states","What users see when something breaks.","Inline (field), banner (system), toast (minor), full screen (fatal). Good errors say what happened, why, and what to do next, in plain words.","“We couldn’t reach your bank. No money was taken. Try again.”","“Every error message answers three things: what happened, is my money safe, what do I do now.”"],
["Partial state","states","Some content loaded, some didn’t; or the user has filled only part.","Mixed success where the UI must show what worked and isolate what failed.","Order loads, but delivery tracking fails.","“If tracking fails, the rest of the order still shows, with only that section marked.”"],
["Success state","states","Confirming clearly that the task is done.","Explicit confirmation with a reference and next step; proportional celebration.","“₹450 paid to Sharma Store. Ref 2311…” with Share receipt.","“The success state doubles as a receipt because users screenshot it as proof.”"],
["Pending state","states","The action is submitted but not finished yet.","Asynchronous processing with expected time, current status and what happens next.","“Refund initiated. Usually reaches your bank in 3–5 days.”","“Pending is the most anxious state, so it needs a time estimate and a way to check.”"],
["Offline state","states","What works and what doesn’t without the internet.","Offline-first: cached reads, queued writes, clear status, sync on reconnect.","Browse your past orders offline; payment waits for network.","“I designed what’s still usable offline instead of just showing ‘No internet’.”"],
["Permission priming","states","Explaining why you need access before the system asks.","A pre-permission screen raises grant rates, because a declined system prompt is hard to re-ask on iOS.","“Allow location so we can show stores that deliver to you in 10 minutes.”","“I prime before the system prompt, because a refusal is almost permanent.”"],
["Locked and paywall state","states","Content that exists but needs an upgrade or permission.","Show the value being locked and the path to unlock; avoid blank or dead-end screens.","A blurred report preview with “Upgrade to see all 12 insights”.","“The locked state should sell the value, not just say no.”"],
["Disabled state","states","A control that exists but can’t be used right now.","Must communicate why it’s disabled and how to enable it; often better to allow tap and explain.","A greyed “Pay” with “Enter an amount to continue” beneath.","“Disabled buttons with no reason are a dead end, so I added the reason.”"],
["Destructive action","states","Deleting, cancelling or other actions that can’t be reversed.","Clear consequences, distinct styling, confirmation for irreversible actions, undo where possible.","“Delete account? Your 3 saved addresses and order history will be removed.”","“The dialog names exactly what will be lost, not just ‘Are you sure?’”"],
["Expired state","states","Links, sessions or offers that are no longer valid.","Timeout handling that preserves user work and offers a recovery path.","“This link has expired. Send a new one.”","“On session expiry we keep the cart, so they only re-login, not restart.”"],
["Content stress testing","states","Checking a design with long, short, missing and weird content.","Test with real data extremes: long names, emoji, RTL scripts, no photo, 9,999+ counts, zero values.","“Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta” in a city field.","“I tested every card with the longest real names in our data, not lorem ipsum.”"],
["Localisation","states","Adapting the product for other languages and regions.","Text expansion (German about 30% longer, Hindi taller line height), RTL mirroring, local formats for date, currency and numbers (lakh and crore).","₹1,50,000 in India versus 150,000 elsewhere.","“The layout leaves room for Hindi and Tamil, and numbers use Indian grouping.”"],
["Graceful degradation","states","When something fails, the experience gets simpler instead of breaking.","Core functionality survives when features, network or devices fall short.","Map fails to load, so the address shows as text.","“If the map fails, the address still shows. Nothing critical depends on it.”"],
["Defensive design","states","Designing for what will go wrong.","Anticipating user errors and system failures; contingency design.","Auto-saving form drafts in case the app closes.","“I design defensively: every input saves as you go.”"],
["UI stack (Scott Hurff)","states","Every screen has five states: ideal, empty, loading, partial, error.","A checklist for completeness that prevents handoffs with only the ideal state.","Designing all five for the orders screen before handoff.","“I hand off the full UI stack for each screen, not just the ideal state.”"],
/* A11Y */
["WCAG","a11y","The global rules for accessible digital products.","Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (currently 2.2). Levels A, AA and AAA; AA is the usual legal and product target.","Most companies commit to WCAG 2.2 AA.","“I design to WCAG 2.2 AA and check contrast, targets and focus order before handoff.”"],
["POUR","a11y","Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust.","The four principles under which WCAG success criteria are organised.","Captions (perceivable), keyboard access (operable), clear errors (understandable), semantic code (robust).","“I check new flows against POUR. Most of our gaps are in operable.”"],
["Contrast ratio","a11y","How readable text is against its background.","WCAG AA: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text (24px or 18.7px bold) and for UI components and focus indicators.","Light grey #9A9A9A on white is about 2.8:1, which fails.","“That placeholder grey fails 4.5:1, so it can’t carry instructions.”"],
["Screen reader","a11y","Software that reads the screen aloud for blind and low-vision users.","VoiceOver (iOS/macOS), TalkBack (Android), NVDA and JAWS (Windows). Relies on semantics, labels and order.","An icon-only button announced as “button” with no name.","“Every icon button needs an accessible name, or VoiceOver just says ‘button’.”"],
["Semantic HTML and ARIA","a11y","Using the right code element so assistive tech understands it.","Native elements (button, nav, h1–h6, label) carry roles; ARIA fills gaps (aria-label, aria-expanded, live regions). First rule of ARIA: don’t use it if native works.","A div styled as a button isn’t keyboard-operable; a real button is.","“I annotate headings and landmarks in the handoff so engineers use the right elements.”"],
["Keyboard navigation and focus order","a11y","Everything works with Tab, Enter and arrows, in a sensible order.","Logical focus order following visual order, no keyboard traps, skip links, focus moved into and back out of modals.","Opening a dialog moves focus into it; closing returns focus to the trigger.","“I specify focus order and where focus goes after a modal closes.”"],
["Focus indicator","a11y","A visible outline showing what’s selected via keyboard.","Must be clearly visible (3:1 contrast) and never removed without a replacement.","A 2px ring around the focused button.","“We can restyle the focus ring, but we can’t remove it.”"],
["Alt text","a11y","A text description of an image for people who can’t see it.","Describes purpose, not appearance; decorative images get empty alt so they’re skipped.","alt=“Delivery partner arriving at your door” versus alt=“image123”.","“Alt text describes what the image does for the user, not every pixel.”"],
["Don’t rely on colour alone","a11y","Never use colour as the only way to show meaning.","About 1 in 12 men has colour vision deficiency. Pair colour with icons, text or patterns.","Red and green order status plus the words “Failed” and “Delivered”.","“Status uses colour and an icon and a word, so it survives colour blindness.”"],
["Touch target size","a11y","Buttons big enough to tap accurately.","Apple 44×44 pt, Material 48×48 dp; WCAG 2.2 minimum 24×24 CSS px with spacing.","A small close icon given a larger invisible hit area.","“The icon is 16 px but its hit area is 44, so it’s easy to tap.”"],
["Reduced motion","a11y","Respecting users who turn off animation.","prefers-reduced-motion media query; replace movement with fades or instant changes for people with vestibular disorders.","Parallax off, cross-fade instead of slide.","“Every animation in the spec has a reduced-motion fallback.”"],
["Dynamic type and text scaling","a11y","Text that grows when users set larger font sizes.","Supporting OS text-size settings (Dynamic Type on iOS, font scale on Android) without truncation or overlap.","Layout reflows when text is set to 200%.","“I checked the layouts at 200% text size; the buttons wrap instead of truncating.”"],
["Inclusive design and persona spectrum","a11y","Designing for the full range of people, including temporary and situational limits.","Microsoft’s persona spectrum: permanent (one arm), temporary (arm injury), situational (holding a baby).","One-handed use helps an amputee, someone injured and a parent.","“Designing for one-handed use helps permanent, temporary and situational cases at once.”"],
["Curb-cut effect","a11y","Features built for disability end up helping everyone.","Accessibility improvements create broad benefits; often the business case for accessibility.","Captions used by people in noisy trains, not just Deaf users.","“Captions are a curb-cut feature: most of our users watch muted.”"],
["Cognitive accessibility","a11y","Making products usable for people with memory, attention or reading difficulties.","Plain language, consistent navigation, no time pressure, clear errors, chunked tasks.","Step-by-step KYC with save-and-resume.","“Plain language and save-and-resume help cognitive accessibility and everyone else.”"],
/* SYSTEMS */
["Design system","systems","A shared set of components, rules and code that keeps a product consistent.","Tokens, components, patterns, guidelines and governance, ideally mirrored in code. More than a UI kit.","Material Design, Atlassian Design System, Razorpay Blade.","“A design system isn’t the Figma file; it’s the agreement between design and code.”"],
["Design tokens","systems","Named values for colour, spacing and type that both design and code use.","Primitive tokens (green-500) feed semantic tokens (color-action-primary), which components reference; enables theming and dark mode.","button.background → color.action.primary → green.600.","“Components use semantic tokens, so dark mode is a token swap, not a redesign.”"],
["Atomic design","systems","Building UI from small parts up: atoms, molecules, organisms, templates, pages.","Brad Frost’s methodology for composing interfaces hierarchically.","Atom: button. Molecule: search field. Organism: header.","“I think in atoms and molecules so new screens are mostly assembly.”"],
["Components and variants","systems","Reusable UI pieces with options like size or state.","Component properties (variant, boolean, instance swap, text) reduce duplication; mirror the code API.","Button with size S/M/L, type primary/secondary, state default/disabled.","“The component props match the engineering API, so handoff is one-to-one.”"],
["Auto layout","systems","Figma’s way of making frames resize like code.","Flexbox-like rules: direction, spacing, padding, hug/fill/fixed sizing, wrap.","A button that grows with its label.","“Auto layout means the design behaves like the built screen when text changes.”"],
["Wireframe, mockup, prototype","systems","Sketch of structure, then visual design, then something clickable.","Fidelity increases: low-fi wireframes for structure, high-fi mockups for visuals, prototypes for interaction testing.","Grey boxes, then final UI, then a clickable Figma flow.","“I tested at wireframe fidelity so feedback was about the flow, not the colours.”"],
["Design handoff","systems","Passing a design to engineering with everything they need.","Specs, tokens, states, annotations, redlines, assets, behaviour notes, accessibility notes and edge cases; increasingly via dev mode and Code Connect.","A Figma page per flow with all states and a notes column.","“My handoff includes every state, the tokens used, and how it behaves on error.”"],
["Annotations","systems","Notes on a design explaining behaviour that pictures can’t show.","Interaction rules, logic, accessibility (headings, labels, order), content rules, analytics events.","“Truncate after 2 lines; full text in tooltip.”","“Annotations cover what the frame can’t: truncation, focus order and analytics events.”"],
["Design QA","systems","Checking the built product against the design before release.","Visual and behavioural review in staging; issues filed by severity.","Spotting that the spacing uses 12 px instead of the 16 px token.","“I do design QA on staging before every release, prioritised by severity.”"],
["Design debt","systems","Accumulated inconsistency and shortcuts in the UI.","Like technical debt: quick decisions that cost more to fix later; tracked and paid down.","Five button styles from five different launches.","“We should budget a sprint per quarter to pay down design debt.”"],
["DesignOps","systems","Making the design team itself run smoothly.","Operations for design: tooling, processes, rituals, hiring, systems governance, research ops.","A shared research repository and file naming conventions.","“Good DesignOps means designers spend time designing, not hunting for files.”"],
["Single source of truth","systems","One authoritative place for a piece of information.","Avoids drift between files, docs and code; often the coded component library.","The Storybook component is the truth; Figma mirrors it.","“If Figma and code disagree, which one is the source of truth?”"],
/* EXPERIMENT */
["A/B test","experiment","Show two versions to different users and see which performs better.","Randomised controlled experiment: control versus variant, one primary metric, pre-set sample size and duration.","Old checkout (A) versus one-page checkout (B), measured on completion rate.","“I’d run it as an A/B test with completion as primary and refund rate as guardrail.”"],
["Statistical significance","experiment","Confidence that a result isn’t just luck.","Usually p < 0.05: less than 5% chance the observed difference came from random variation if there were no real effect.","Variant B is +2% but not significant after three days, so keep running.","“It’s not significant yet, so I wouldn’t call it a win.”"],
["Sample size and MDE","experiment","How many users you need to detect a change of a certain size.","Minimum detectable effect: smaller expected effects need far larger samples. Decide before launching, don’t peek.","Detecting a 1% lift may need hundreds of thousands of users.","“With our traffic we can’t detect a 1% change, so this is a qualitative call.”"],
["Novelty effect","experiment","People engage with something new just because it’s new.","Short-term lift that fades; run tests long enough to see past it.","A redesigned home screen spikes clicks for a week.","“Week one is inflated by novelty, so I’d judge it at week three.”"],
["Multivariate test","experiment","Testing several changes at once to find the best combination.","Tests interactions between variables; needs much more traffic than A/B.","Headline × image × button text, eight combinations.","“We don’t have the traffic for multivariate, so I’d test the headline alone first.”"],
["Feature flag","experiment","A switch that turns a feature on or off for some users without a new release.","Enables gradual rollouts, targeting, quick rollback and experiments.","New checkout enabled for 5% of Android users.","“We ship behind a flag and widen the rollout as the metrics hold.”"],
["Staged rollout / canary","experiment","Release to a small group first, then everyone.","Progressive exposure to limit blast radius of problems.","1%, then 10%, then 50%, then 100% over a week.","“A canary release catches the problems that only real traffic reveals.”"],
["Fake door test","experiment","Show a button for a feature that doesn’t exist yet, and count the taps.","Measures demand before building; tapping explains the feature is coming. Use sparingly to avoid frustrating users.","A “Split bill” button that leads to “Coming soon, want early access?”","“A fake door told us 8% would try it before we spent a sprint.”"],
["Wizard of Oz test","experiment","A human secretly does the work the product will automate.","Simulates functionality to test the experience before building the technology.","Recommendations chosen by a person behind the scenes.","“We ran the AI stylist as Wizard of Oz first to see if people valued the advice.”"],
["Concierge MVP","experiment","Delivering the service by hand, openly, to learn.","Manual, visible service for a few customers before automating.","Personally ordering groceries over WhatsApp for 20 households.","“A concierge MVP showed us which steps people actually cared about automating.”"],
["Five-second, first-click, preference tests","experiment","Quick tests of first impressions, where people tap first, and which version they prefer.","Five-second: recall and clarity. First-click: findability predicts task success. Preference: opinion, weakest evidence.","Show the landing page for five seconds: “What does this company do?”","“Preference tests tell us what people like, not what works. I pair them with a first-click test.”"],
/* GROWTH */
["CAC","growth","How much it costs to get one new customer.","Customer Acquisition Cost: total sales and marketing spend ÷ new customers acquired, per channel.","₹10 lakh spent, 5,000 customers: CAC ₹200.","“Better referral design lowers CAC because referred users are cheaper.”"],
["LTV (lifetime value)","growth","How much a customer is worth over their whole relationship with you.","Average revenue per user × gross margin × expected lifetime (or ÷ churn rate).","₹150/month margin, 20-month life: LTV ₹3,000.","“Retention work raises LTV, which is what makes the acquisition spend worth it.”"],
["LTV:CAC ratio","growth","Is each customer worth more than they cost to get?","Healthy is often 3:1 or better; below 1:1 means losing money on every customer.","LTV ₹3,000, CAC ₹600: 5:1.","“At 1.5:1 the problem isn’t growth, it’s retention.”"],
["ARPU and AOV","growth","Average revenue per user; average order value.","ARPU = revenue ÷ users in a period. AOV = revenue ÷ orders. Quick-commerce lives on AOV versus delivery cost.","AOV ₹450 with ₹60 delivery cost.","“Basket-building suggestions raise AOV, but I’d guard against annoying first-time users.”"],
["GMV","growth","Total value of everything sold on a platform.","Gross Merchandise Value before returns, discounts and fees; a marketplace scale metric, not revenue.","A marketplace with ₹100 crore GMV and 15% take rate earns ₹15 crore.","“GMV can grow from discounts alone, so I’d watch contribution margin too.”"],
["MRR and ARR","growth","Monthly and annual recurring revenue from subscriptions.","Predictable revenue; expansion, contraction and churn MRR explain its movement.","200 users × ₹499 = ₹99,800 MRR.","“Most of our MRR loss is contraction, people downgrading, so the plan page needs work.”"],
["Freemium and paywall","growth","Free core product, paid extras; the paywall is where free stops.","Paywall placement trades conversion against activation; soft (limits) versus hard (blocks).","Spotify free with ads; Premium for offline and no ads.","“The paywall should come after the aha moment, never before it.”"],
["Free trial (opt-in vs opt-out)","growth","Try free; opt-in asks for a card later, opt-out takes it upfront.","Opt-out trials convert more but attract fewer sign-ups and more resentment; disclosure matters.","Seven-day trial with a reminder two days before charging.","“Sending a reminder before the trial ends costs a little conversion and buys a lot of trust.”"],
["Cohort analysis","growth","Grouping users by when they joined and comparing their behaviour over time.","Separates product improvements from mix changes; standard for retention.","January sign-ups retain 30% at day 30; March sign-ups 38% after the new onboarding.","“Comparing cohorts shows the new onboarding lifted day-30 retention.”"],
["Aha moment","growth","The moment a new user first gets why the product is valuable.","The action most correlated with long-term retention; onboarding’s job is to get users there fast.","Facebook’s classic: 7 friends in 10 days.","“Our aha moment is the first order arriving early, so onboarding should get them to order.”"],
["Activation","growth","A new user doing the key thing that predicts they’ll stay.","Defined behaviourally (completed first X within Y days); the bridge between acquisition and retention.","Activated: completed first payment within 7 days.","“I’d define activation as first successful payment, then design backward from that.”"],
["FTUE","growth","First Time User Experience: the first minutes in the product.","Includes sign-up, permissions, onboarding and the first task; disproportionate influence on retention.","Sign-up to first order in under two minutes.","“The FTUE asks for five permissions upfront. I’d ask each one in context.”"],
["Time to value","growth","How fast a new user gets real value.","Duration from sign-up to first meaningful outcome; shorter correlates with activation.","Letting users browse before forcing login.","“Delaying login until checkout cut time to value in half.”"],
["Network effect","growth","The product gets more valuable as more people use it.","Direct (users to users, like WhatsApp) or indirect / two-sided (buyers to sellers, like Swiggy).","More restaurants attract more diners, which attracts more restaurants.","“For a two-sided marketplace, I’d design the supply side’s onboarding first.”"],
["Flywheel","growth","A self-reinforcing growth loop.","Amazon’s flywheel: lower prices → more customers → more sellers → more selection → lower costs.","More reviews → better recommendations → more purchases → more reviews.","“This feature feeds the review flywheel, which is why it’s worth prioritising.”"],
["Viral coefficient (K-factor)","growth","How many new users each user brings in.","K = invites sent per user × conversion of invites. K > 1 means self-sustaining growth.","Each user invites 4 people, 30% join: K = 1.2.","“The referral flow’s weak point is invite conversion, so I’d fix the landing page for invitees.”"],
["Product-led growth (PLG)","growth","The product itself sells the product.","Users discover, adopt and pay through the product experience rather than a sales team.","Figma: free to start, shareable files invite colleagues.","“In PLG, the design of sharing and onboarding is the go-to-market.”"],
["Switching cost","growth","How painful it is to leave for a competitor.","Data, habits, integrations and learning that make leaving costly; a retention moat. Can be ethical (value) or not (lock-in).","Years of photos and playlists.","“Saved lists create healthy switching costs, value that stays with us.”"],
["Unit economics","growth","Whether you make or lose money on each order or customer.","Revenue minus variable costs per unit (order, user); determines whether growth helps or hurts.","An order makes ₹40 margin but costs ₹55 to deliver.","“Free delivery on small orders breaks the unit economics, so the minimum-order nudge exists for a reason.”"],
/* TECH */
["API","tech","How the app asks the server for data or actions.","Application Programming Interface: defined requests and responses (REST, GraphQL). The data available shapes what design is possible.","The app calls /orders/123 and gets status, items and ETA.","“Does the API return refund ETA, or do we need a backend change for this design?”"],
["Frontend and backend","tech","Frontend is what users see; backend is the servers and data behind it.","Frontend renders UI and handles interaction; backend handles logic, storage, integrations and security.","A loading delay might be backend speed, not UI.","“The animation is frontend work; the status data needs a backend change.”"],
["Client-side vs server-side","tech","Whether the user’s device or a remote server does the work.","Affects speed, offline capability, security and battery. Validation is often done on both.","Formatting a phone number on device; checking it is registered on the server.","“We can validate format client-side for instant feedback, then confirm server-side.”"],
["Latency","tech","The delay between asking and getting an answer.","Network round-trip plus processing time; design hides it with optimistic UI, skeletons and caching.","A tap that takes two seconds to respond on 3G.","“On 3G latency is unavoidable, so the UI must acknowledge the tap instantly.”"],
["Caching","tech","Saving data locally so it doesn’t need to be fetched again.","Stores responses for reuse; trade-off between freshness and speed (stale-while-revalidate).","Show yesterday’s order list instantly, then refresh.","“With caching the screen opens instantly and updates quietly.”"],
["Technical debt","tech","Code shortcuts that make future changes slower.","Deferred engineering quality; interest paid in slower delivery and bugs.","A hard-coded colour value in 200 places.","“That design change is simple, but tech debt makes it a two-week job, so let’s scope it.”"],
["Native, web, cross-platform","tech","Apps built for one platform, for browsers, or once for both.","Native (Swift, Kotlin) gives best performance and platform feel; cross-platform (React Native, Flutter) shares code; PWA runs in the browser.","A Flutter app must still respect iOS back-swipe.","“Even in Flutter we should keep platform conventions like back navigation.”"],
["Deep link","tech","A link that opens a specific screen inside an app.","Universal links and app links route to content, with fallback to web or store.","A notification opens directly on the order’s tracking page.","“The notification should deep link to the order, not the home screen.”"],
["Push notification","tech","A message from the app that appears even when it’s closed.","Requires permission; types: transactional, promotional, behavioural. Over-sending drives opt-outs and uninstalls.","“Your order is 2 minutes away.”","“Transactional pushes earn trust; promotional ones spend it.”"],
["Event tracking (instrumentation)","tech","Recording user actions so they can be measured.","Analytics events with properties (name, screen, values) defined in a tracking plan; without them you can’t measure the design.","checkout_step_viewed {step: address}.","“I add the analytics events to the handoff so we can measure the redesign from day one.”"],
["Edge network and CDN","tech","Servers spread around the world that deliver files quickly.","A Content Delivery Network caches images and assets near users to cut load time.","Product images load from a server in Mumbai, not the US.","“Image weight matters more than anything else for our 4G users, so the CDN should serve compressed formats.”"],
/* AI */
["Human-in-the-loop","ai","A person reviews or approves what the AI does.","Human oversight at decision points; level depends on risk and reversibility of the action.","AI drafts the reply; the agent edits and sends it.","“For anything involving money, I’d keep a human-in-the-loop confirmation.”"],
["Hallucination","ai","When AI states something false with confidence.","Model output not grounded in source data. Design must expect it: citations, verification, easy correction.","A support bot inventing a refund policy.","“We design assuming the model will sometimes be wrong, so every answer links to its source.”"],
["Grounding and citations","ai","Showing where the AI’s answer came from.","Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) bases answers on trusted documents; citations let users verify.","“Based on your last 3 orders [view]”.","“Citations turn ‘trust me’ into ‘check me’, which is what builds trust.”"],
["Confidence and uncertainty UI","ai","Showing how sure the AI is.","Signals of model certainty (labels, ranges, alternatives) help users calibrate trust; avoid false precision.","“Likely match” versus “Exact match” in a search.","“Showing uncertainty prevents over-trust when the model guesses.”"],
["Trust calibration","ai","Helping users trust AI exactly as much as it deserves.","Avoiding both over-reliance and under-use through transparency, track record and correction.","Showing when the AI was right before, and making errors easy to flag.","“The goal isn’t maximum trust, it’s calibrated trust.”"],
["Explainability","ai","Telling users why the AI made a choice.","Interpretable reasons for recommendations or decisions; legally required in some domains like credit.","“Recommended because you bought rice last week.”","“A one-line reason makes a recommendation feel helpful instead of creepy.”"],
["Agentic UX and autonomy levels","ai","Designing for AI that takes actions, not just answers.","Spectrum from suggest → draft → act with approval → act and report. Needs previews, permissions, activity logs and undo.","An agent that reorders weekly groceries, showing the cart before paying.","“I’d start the agent at ‘act with approval’ and earn the right to full autonomy.”"],
["Streaming responses","ai","Showing AI text as it’s generated instead of waiting.","Reduces perceived latency; needs stop control, stable layout and a clear done state.","Chat answers appearing word by word.","“Streaming hides model latency, but users need a stop button.”"],
["Prompt affordances","ai","Helping users know what to ask an AI.","Starter prompts, examples, suggested follow-ups and structured inputs solve the blank-box problem.","“Try: plan a week of vegetarian dinners under ₹2,000.”","“An empty prompt box has no affordance, so I seed it with real examples.”"],
["Guardrails (AI)","ai","Limits that stop AI doing or saying harmful things.","Policy filters, scope limits, refusal handling and safe fallbacks designed into the flow.","A banking assistant refuses to move money without confirmation.","“The guardrail UX matters too. A refusal should explain and offer a path.”"],
["AI feedback loop and evals","ai","Collecting user ratings and testing AI quality systematically.","Thumbs, corrections and regenerations feed evaluation sets; evals measure quality before release.","Thumbs-down with “What was wrong?” options.","“Thumbs-down data feeds our evals, so feedback design directly improves the model.”"],
/* ETHICS */
["Dark patterns (deceptive design)","ethics","Design tricks that push users into things they didn’t mean to do.","Harry Brignull’s taxonomy; increasingly regulated (India’s CCPA guidelines on dark patterns, 2023; EU DSA).","Pre-ticked add-on insurance at checkout.","“That’s a dark pattern, and in India it’s now a regulatory risk too, not just an ethical one.”"],
["Confirmshaming","ethics","Guilt-tripping people for saying no.","Opt-out copy that shames the user into compliance.","“No thanks, I don’t like saving money.”","“A neutral ‘No thanks’ costs a little conversion and keeps our dignity.”"],
["Roach motel","ethics","Easy to get in, hard to get out.","Sign-up in one tap, cancellation hidden or requiring a call.","Cancel only by emailing support.","“Cancel should be as easy as subscribe. Anything else hurts us long-term.”"],
["Drip pricing and hidden costs","ethics","Fees that appear only at the last step.","Partial pricing upfront with mandatory charges revealed late; listed among India’s specified dark patterns.","Convenience fee added on the payment screen.","“Showing all fees on the cart screen lowers surprise drop-off at payment.”"],
["Basket sneaking","ethics","Adding things to the cart the user didn’t choose.","Pre-added items, tips or donations without explicit consent.","A ₹2 donation added by default.","“Opt-in, not opt-out, for anything that costs the user money.”"],
["Forced continuity","ethics","A free trial that quietly turns into a paid plan.","Automatic billing without clear notice or easy cancellation.","Charged after the trial with no reminder.","“A reminder before billing is the honest version of the same trial.”"],
["Privacy by design","ethics","Building privacy in from the start, not adding it later.","Data minimisation, clear consent, sensible defaults, purpose limitation; India’s DPDP Act 2023 sets consent rules.","Asking for contacts only when the user chooses to invite friends.","“We only ask for data at the moment it’s needed and say why.”"],
["Attention economy","ethics","User attention is limited and companies compete to capture it.","Business models that monetise engagement create pressure toward compulsive design.","Autoplay and infinite feeds.","“Engagement isn’t the goal for us; completing the task fast is.”"],
["Digital wellbeing","ethics","Designing to protect people from overuse.","Usage summaries, quiet hours, notification controls, natural stopping points.","“You’re all caught up.”","“A natural stopping point respects the user and still brings them back tomorrow.”"],
/* SENIOR */
["Problem framing","senior","Defining the right problem before solving it.","Clarifying users, goals, constraints and success before ideation; reframing when the brief describes a solution.","Brief: “add a chatbot”. Frame: “users can’t find refund status”.","“Before I solve it, can I check the problem? The brief describes a solution.”"],
["Trade-off","senior","What you give up to get something else.","Explicit choices between competing goals (speed vs accuracy, simplicity vs power). Seniors name them out loud.","One-page checkout is faster but harder to scan on small phones.","“The trade-off I chose was fewer options for faster checkout, and here’s how I’d know if it was wrong.”"],
["Constraints","senior","The limits you design within: time, tech, legal, business.","Constraints sharpen design; stating them shows judgement.","Must ship in two sprints, RBI rules require 2FA, no backend changes.","“Given two sprints and no backend changes, the best version is…”"],
["Design rationale","senior","The reason behind each decision.","Linking choices to evidence, principles and goals so others can evaluate them, not just like or dislike.","“Bottom placement because 80% use one hand, and testing showed faster completion.”","“Every decision in this deck has its reason next to it.”"],
["Outcome over output","senior","Judging work by the change it causes, not what was shipped.","Outputs are features and screens; outcomes are behaviour change and business results.","Output: new onboarding. Outcome: activation up 12%.","“The deliverable was the flow; the outcome was a 12% lift in activation.”"],
["Success criteria","senior","How everyone will know the work succeeded, agreed in advance.","Measurable targets defined before design or launch.","Refund-status tickets down 30% within a month.","“Before we start, what does success look like, and how will we measure it?”"],
["Alignment","senior","Getting the team to agree on goals and direction.","Shared understanding across design, product, engineering and business; achieved through framing, evidence and early involvement.","A kick-off where PM, engineering and design agree the problem.","“I involve engineering in early sketches so we’re aligned on feasibility before polish.”"],
["Disagree and commit","senior","Voice your disagreement, then fully support the decision once made.","Amazon principle that keeps teams moving after debate.","You prefer option A, team picks B; you make B excellent.","“I argued for A with data, the team went with B, and I committed to making B work.”"],
["Ambiguity","senior","Situations without clear answers or requirements.","Comfort with ambiguity is a seniority signal: making progress by framing, testing and deciding with incomplete information.","A vague brief like “improve engagement”.","“When the brief is ambiguous, I narrow it with a hypothesis and test quickly.”"],
["Second-order effects","senior","The consequences of the consequences.","Downstream effects on other metrics, teams or users beyond the immediate change.","Free delivery increases orders, but also tiny orders that lose money.","“The second-order effect is more small orders, so I’d add a minimum-basket guardrail.”"],
["First principles","senior","Reasoning from basic truths, not from what others do.","Breaking a problem into fundamentals before rebuilding a solution.","What does a payment confirmation fundamentally need to prove?","“From first principles, the user needs proof that money moved, so the receipt is the core.”"],
["Systems thinking","senior","Seeing how parts connect and affect each other.","Considering flows, data, teams and incentives as an interconnected system rather than isolated screens.","A checkout change affects support load, warehouse picking and refunds.","“This isn’t a screen problem, it’s a system problem across three teams.”"],
["Scope slicing","senior","Cutting a big idea into smaller shippable parts.","Thin vertical slices that each deliver user value and learning.","Ship refund status first, then refund chat later.","“I sliced it so the first release ships in two weeks and teaches us what to build next.”"],
["Signal vs noise","senior","Separating meaningful evidence from random variation.","Judging data quality, sample size and consistency before acting.","One loud user complaint versus a pattern in 300 tickets.","“One stakeholder anecdote is noise; 300 tickets is signal.”"],
["Craft","senior","The quality of execution: detail, polish and care.","Visual, interaction and content quality; seniors balance craft with speed and impact.","Perfect alignment, real copy, every state designed.","“I spend craft where users feel it most: the payment moment, not the settings page.”"],
/* MARKET */
["Next billion users","market","The large wave of people coming online for the first time, mostly on budget phones.","Users with lower digital literacy, low-end Android, intermittent data, and voice- and video-first habits.","A first-time smartphone user in a tier-3 town.","“Designing for next billion users means fewer words, more recognition, and voice support.”"],
["Vernacular-first design","market","Designing in local languages from the start, not translating later.","Hindi, Tamil, Bengali and more have different lengths, scripts and line heights; mixed language (Hinglish) is common.","Meesho and ShareChat built in regional languages first.","“I check every layout in Hindi and Tamil, not just English, because line height differs.”"],
["Low-bandwidth design","market","Making the app work well on slow or patchy internet.","Light pages, compressed images, offline caches, retry handling and clear network states.","A 2G-friendly lite mode.","“Image weight is our biggest UX problem on 3G, so I’d design a lighter list view.”"],
["UPI flows","market","India’s instant payment system and its user journeys.","Intent flow (opens UPI app), collect request, QR scan, UPI Lite, autopay mandates; failure and pending states are common and anxiety-inducing.","A payment stuck as “pending” for 30 minutes.","“The hardest UPI state is pending, so it needs a clear ETA and automatic refund messaging.”"],
["Trust signals","market","Cues that make an unfamiliar product feel safe.","Cash on delivery, recognisable payment logos, clear refund policy, support access, real reviews.","COD still matters for many first-time online buyers.","“For first-time buyers, COD and a visible refund promise do more than any visual polish.”"],
["Quick-commerce ETA promise","market","The delivery-time promise that defines apps like Blinkit, Zepto and Instamart.","The ETA is the product: accuracy, honest delays and recovery when the promise breaks are core UX.","“Arriving in 9 minutes” that becomes 25 minutes.","“A broken ETA promise needs proactive recovery, not a silent timer.”"]
];
